# Model tiers, workers and escalation

This project uses two model settings (`project.toml`, `[tiers]`): a **strong tier**
({{tiers.strong}}) and a **worker tier** ({{tiers.worker}}). This doc says what each one does,
how work moves between them, and how a worker hands back.

## Why two tiers

Direction and execution succeed in different ways. Direction (which route, what to try
next, is this result what it seems) is judged later and is expensive to get wrong.
Execution (does this lemma type-check, does this script reproduce the known value) has a
fast, mechanical success signal. So the strong tier directs and reviews, and the worker
tier executes, checked by that mechanical signal.

## Routing rules

- **The session's tier follows its shape, decided up front.** A design session (choosing a
  route, decomposing a hard step, deciding what a result means) runs on the strong tier.
  A session that only works through an already-written brick list may run on the worker
  tier while the owner ({{owner}}) is present. An unattended stretch runs as a strong coordinator
  dispatching workers, so that every escalation goes to the coordinator, not to the owner.
  *Why:* a cheap model directing itself escalates to the owner, who then becomes the
  bottleneck.
- **Do not switch models in the middle of a session.** *Why:* the switch throws away the
  cached context, and the session pays for it again at the new price. Only the owner can
  switch a session's model. In a worker-tier session the owner runs, NEED-DIRECTION goes to
  the owner; the design continues in a strong-tier session started from the written state.
- **Dispatch whole bricks.** A worker starts cold and spends effort reading in; a one-line
  fix does not repay that. A brick is something with its own finish line: a lemma group, a
  script plus its validation, a table.
- **Reviewers never run below the tier of the author they review.** Their agent files pin
  them to the strong tier (`tier: strong` in the source, the strong model in
  {{#if harness.is_claude}}`.claude/agents/`{{else}}`.codex/agents/`{{/if}}), so a dispatch cannot lower it. *Why:* the review is the
  signal everything else trusts; it is cheap per run, so there is nothing to save.
- **A worker-tier session that meets a design question returns NEED-DIRECTION.** It does not
  pick a route, even when it feels sure. *Why:* choosing a route is the strong tier's job;
  a cheaper model that picks its own makes exactly the decision the split exists to keep
  from it.
- **Analysis, record wording and the brick list itself stay on the strong tier.**

*Anti-scope:* none of this forbids the coordinator a small inline edit that needs the
surrounding context; it forbids grinding (repeated attempts, writing whole scripts) inline
when a worker could take the unit.

## The coordinator owns every long job

A **long job** is anything that may run past about two minutes{{#if modules.experiments}} (the default smoke-run limit in `[experiments.limits]` is 120 s){{/if}}: a full build, a sweep over
many cases, a timing probe over representative inputs. Only the coordinator starts one.
*Anti-scope:* a seconds-scale check on one small case is not a long job; workers run those.

*Why:* a dispatched agent's turn ends when it returns. A job it started keeps running with
nobody waiting for its result (or dies with the agent), and the result is lost. No hook
can reliably tell a worker's command from the coordinator's, so the rule lives in two
places. The worker's charter is the `worker` agent files{{#if modules.lean}} (also `lean-worker`){{/if}}{{#if modules.experiments}} (also `exp-worker`){{/if}}; the
coordinator writes the same clause into every brief it sends: **validate on one
seconds-scale known-answer case, then return the exact command.** The hook `hooks/agent-brief-lint.py` nudges when a brief
asks an agent to run or time a long job.
{{#if modules.launch}}
How the coordinator then runs it: [launch.md](launch.md).
{{/if}}

## How a worker ends: DONE, PARTIAL, NEED-DIRECTION

- `DONE`: the brick landed; the return names the check run and shows its raw output.
- `PARTIAL`: what landed, what did not, and the remaining bricks as a numbered list.
- `NEED-DIRECTION`: an escalation trigger fired. The return carries the trigger, the
  current state, each attempt, and the routes the worker sees, **without picking one**.

On a proof or construction brick the return carries a `CLAIM:` line (`PROVED`,
`COUNTEREXAMPLE`, `GAPS`, `ARGUED`, `NOT-PROVED`, `NONE`; the charter's table). **Copy it
verbatim** into your report and the record: a reworded verdict loses what was claimed, and an
`ARGUED` claim's labels and premises are what keep it from being read as a result. The same
goes for any report to the owner: it repeats the record's hedge ("heuristically", "on n ≤
50", "conditional on …"), never a stronger summary of it. `NOT-PROVED` records that three
different attempts failed; it is not evidence that the statement is false, and it never
moves a conjecture's status.

Every return also carries the exact commands run and raw values next to any pass/fail
derived from them, so the coordinator can re-check instead of trusting a summary.

## Escalation triggers (mechanical on purpose)

An **attempt** is one edit-plus-check cycle: change something, run the check, read the
result. A worker stops and returns NEED-DIRECTION when:

1. the same goal survives 3 attempts, each genuinely different;
2. the same build or run error survives 2 attempts unchanged (a special case of 1 that
   fires earlier);
3. there are two or more possible routes and the brief did not choose;
4. something it needs (a lemma, a function, a reference) is missing after one search;
5. a known-answer check still fails after 1 fix attempt{{#if modules.experiments}} (the `exp-worker` gets two:
   passing that check is what its driver is built for){{/if}};
6. a resource limit trips;
7. a result contradicts the brief, the project's records or an earlier run;
8. it is about to write a judgment of difficulty, scope or impossibility, or any sentence
   bound for a durable record.

If two triggers apply, whichever fires first wins; do not wait for the others.

*Why mechanical:* a model is a poor judge of its own stuckness. Left to judge, it either
grinds for hours or quits at the first obstacle. A counted trigger does neither.

## Answering NEED-DIRECTION

The coordinator decides; it does not take the grinding over.
{{#if harness.cap.resume_subagent}}
Answer by messaging the **same** worker, which keeps its context, rather than dispatching a
fresh one that must read everything in again.
{{else}}
Dispatch a fresh worker with the previous report and your decision attached; the report is
written to be complete enough to restart from.
{{/if}}
Once answered, the work continues on the worker tier.
{{#if modules.attempts}}
When the answer is a choice of route on a hard target, load the `attack` skill: it lists the
approaches and moves, and records the chosen line with `bin/attempts new`.
{{/if}}

**A stuck search is answered by breadth (best-of-n); correctness is decided by a step
audit.**{{#if modules.attempts}} (principle: best-of-n-when-stuck){{/if}} When the problem is *finding* something (the right lemma, an approach that works,
candidate conjectures), dispatch several attempts with deliberately different approaches
and keep the best: sampling widens the set of ideas tried. But whether an argument is
*correct* is decided by checking it step by step (a reviewer), never by a vote across
attempts: several attempts can agree on an answer while each hides a broken step.
*Anti-scope:* best-of-n is for a search that is genuinely stuck, not the first attempt at
every brick.
{{#if modules.attempts}}
Each parallel or restarted attempt is briefed with three things: the problem, the
question's examples bank, and `bin/attempts --obstructions <target>` under "Already ruled
out". Not the failed transcripts: shown attempts anchor the next one on the same route.
Withholding them is a judgment, not yet measured; say in the record which inputs a restart
got.
{{/if}}

## Keeping the coordinator's context small

The coordinator's context is the expensive one: it is re-read on every turn. Keep durable
state on disk (progress logs, the records, commits) and keep worker returns short (a
headline, the evidence, the command). After the context is summarized or reset, rebuild
the state from those files and from the recent commits, not from memory of the summary.

## Briefing an agent

Templates (copy, fill, delete what does not apply):

- [dispatcher-brief.md](../templates/dispatcher-brief.md): a strong coordinator taking
  over several independent threads (orient, fan out, verify, record).
- [worker-handoff.md](../templates/worker-handoff.md): a worker working through a
  brick list while the owner is present.
- [agent-brief.md](../templates/agent-brief.md): one fresh agent, one bounded task, run
  to completion in one turn.
- [subagent-brief-checklist.md](../templates/subagent-brief-checklist.md): the
  guardrails to paste into any brief.

When a brief asks the agent to *find something new*, list what is already known not to be
new: earlier negative results **and** known symmetries or trivial cases of the object.
*Why:* an agent with no context rediscovers a known symmetry and reports it as a finding.

## Asking the owner

Before asking the owner to pick one of several next steps, check whether two or more of
them are independent, cheap threads within the task you were given. If so, do them (in
parallel where you can), report, and stop at the end of the task, keeping only the real
choice as a one-line note. Ask only at a genuine fork: mutually exclusive designs, an
irreversible or outward-facing call, or a scope decision only the owner can make. Starting
a new research direction needs a yes unless there is a continue signal (the owner's most
recent message says keep going, or a standing mandate they have not withdrawn).{{#if modules.stopping}} Full
rules: [stopping.md](stopping.md).{{/if}} What a task leaves unspecified is a different matter: before a
long computation or a new line of work, the
intake check (the coordinator's ground rules) takes each answer from
`notes/procedures.md` and asks the owner about the rest, in one question.
{{#if harness.cap.ask_tool}}
The hook `hooks/askquestion-fanout-nudge.py` nudges once when a structured question offers a
single choice among three or more options.
{{/if}}

## Measuring whether this works

When a reviewer rejects a worker's output, or you redo it, note it{{#if modules.reflect}} as friction (see
the reflection loop){{/if}} with the worker's tier. If the worker tier's redo rate climbs,
or it fails silently (a passing check that hid a wrong result), move that kind of work back
to the strong tier.
